course: everyday
lesson: 36
title: Calling emergency services well
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An emergency is when someone's life, health or home is in danger right now and help must come fast: a fire, a person who will not wake up or cannot breathe, a bad injury, or a crime happening.
explain: Each country has one short number for police, fire and ambulance: 911 in the USA and Canada, 999 in the UK and Ireland, 000 in Australia and 111 in New Zealand. A call to it is free.
explain: The person who answers asks which service you need and then asks questions. You answer clearly: what happened, where you are, and who is hurt. The address is the most important thing, because help cannot come if nobody knows where.
explain: For example, a boy says: "My grandma fell and won't wake up. We're at 14 Oak Street, Denver, the blue house." Then he stays on the line, does what he is told, and does not hang up until the call taker says so.
explain: If you do not know the address, you describe what you can see: a shop name, a street sign, a park or a big building nearby. Mobile phones can usually call the emergency number even when they are locked.
explain: A girl in Texas called 911 when her grandpa collapsed but did not know her address; it took the ambulance longer to find the house.
explain: Prank calls to 999 in England tied up the line while real people needed help, and the callers' families were visited by police.
fact: The emergency number is 911 in the USA and Canada, 999 in the UK and Ireland, 000 in Australia and 111 in New Zealand.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, 112 also reaches the emergency services.
fact: Calls to the emergency number are free.
fact: The most important thing to tell the call taker is where you are.
fact: You should also say what happened and how many people are hurt.
fact: You should stay on the line until the call taker tells you to hang up.
fact: If you do not know the address, you describe landmarks such as shops, signs or parks.
fact: Most mobile phones can call the emergency number even when they are locked.
fact: Calling the emergency number as a joke is wrong and can stop real emergencies from getting help.
fact: If you call the emergency number by mistake, you should stay on the line and say it was a mistake.
fact: A lost toy, a broken game or a small scrape is not an emergency.
fact: Prank or silly calls to emergency numbers can stop someone in real danger getting help.
fact: Knowing your address helps an ambulance or fire engine reach you faster.
exercise: What is the emergency number in Australia? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
exercise: What is the emergency number in New Zealand? | 111 | 000 | 999 | 911
exercise: What is the most important thing to tell the emergency call taker? | Where you are | Your full name and age | What school you go to | What your parents do for work
exercise: When should you hang up an emergency call? | When the call taker tells you to | As soon as you have said the address | As soon as you hear the siren | After about ten seconds
exercise: Which of these is a real emergency? | A man has collapsed and is not breathing | Your bike has a flat tyre and you need it for school | You cannot find your homework | The TV has stopped working
exercise: You call 911 by accident. What should you do? | Stay on the line and say it was a mistake | Hang up quickly and hope they forget about it | Turn off the phone and hide it | Call back again and again to explain
exercise: You are at a park and do not know the address. What can you tell the call taker? | Landmarks you can see, like a shop or street sign | Nothing, they cannot help without an address | The address of your school instead | Just that you are somewhere outside
exercise: Why is it wrong to call the emergency number as a joke? | It can delay help for someone in real danger | It costs the caller a lot of money | It makes the phone stop working for a whole week | It is only wrong if you are an adult
exercise: Your mum's phone is locked and she has fainted. What can you try? | The emergency call button on the locked screen | Guessing the password many times | Waiting until she wakes up to unlock it for you | Looking for a different phone in town
exercise: A girl in Manchester sees smoke pouring out of the house next door. What should she do? | Call 999 and ask for the fire service | Run inside to warn the people | Post a video of it online first so people can see | Wait to see if the smoke stops
exercise: Why should you speak slowly and clearly on an emergency call? | So the call taker understands the details correctly | So the call is cheaper to make | So the ambulance drives more slowly and carefully | So the call lasts as long as possible
exercise: Which three things should you tell the call taker? | What happened, where, and who is hurt | Your age, your school and your teacher | The weather, the time and the date | Your phone model and its battery level
exercise: Why does the call taker keep you on the line after help is sent? | They may give you instructions to help until help arrives | They want to chat to keep you company | The call must last a set number of minutes | They need to check the phone line is working
exercise: In Canada, which number do you call when someone is badly hurt? | 911 | 999 | 000 | 111
test: A boy in Perth, Australia, finds his dad on the floor, not answering. What should he do? | Call 000 and say what happened and where | Call 911 because it is on television | Wait until his mum comes home | Send his friend a message asking what to do
test: Ann calls 999 and says, "Come quick, Grandad is hurt!" and then hangs up. What went wrong? | She did not say where she was or stay on the line | She called the wrong number for the UK | She spoke too slowly and quietly for the call taker | Nothing, help will find her anyway
test: Which is NOT a reason to call the emergency number? | Your little brother took your pencil | A car has crashed and someone is bleeding | A stranger is breaking into a house | Your friend has stopped breathing
test: A girl in Seattle is on the phone with 911 and hears the ambulance outside. What should she do? | Stay on the line until the call taker says she can hang up | Hang up at once and run outside to wave at the ambulance | Turn off the phone to save battery | Call her friend to tell her the news
test: A family on holiday in Wellington, New Zealand, sees a fire. Which number should they dial? | 111 | 911 | 999 | 000
test: Tom is lost in a big city park when his friend falls and breaks a leg. What helps the ambulance find them? | Saying the park's name and what he can see nearby | Saying only that they are in a park | Waiting for the ambulance to come by without calling anyone | Saying his home address instead
test: What would most likely happen if lots of people made joke calls to the emergency number? | Real callers would wait longer for help | Ambulances would drive faster everywhere | The number would be changed every day | Nothing, because joke calls are ignored
test: Which statement is true? | You do not have to pay to call the emergency number | Only adults are allowed to call the emergency number | You must unlock a phone before calling for help | The emergency number is the same in every country
test: A child in Galway, Ireland, sees a man fall into the river. Which numbers could she call? | 999 or 112 | 911 or 000 | 111 or 000 | 000 or 911
test: nan fell down and won't wake up, called 999, what do i say first | Where you are | What you had for lunch | Your favourite colour | Your teacher's name
test: dont know the address, we're at some park | Describe landmarks like shops and signs | Hang up and try later | Make up an address | Say anywhere is fine, they'll find you with your phone
test: when can i hang up | When the call taker says to | Straight after saying hello | As soon as you've said where | When you hear a siren
test: called 911 by accident oops | Stay on the line and say it was a mistake | Hang up quickly | Turn your phone off | Call again and hang up
test: dared to prank call 911 lol | No, it can stop someone in danger getting help | Yes, they will think it is funny and laugh about it too | Yes, if you hang up fast | Yes, if you use a friend's phone
test: called 000 and i'm scared, can i hang up now | Stay on the line until they say you can go | Hang up as soon as you have said what happened | Hang up and call your friend | Hang up and call again later
test: Ethan says they find u by magic so u don't need ur address. true? | No, say your address clearly so they come fast | Yes, they always know where you are | Yes, phones send it to them perfectly every time | Yes, unless you live in a city
test: did u hear some kid prank called 999 and the police came to his school and arrested him lol | Real lesson: prank calls are wrong and block real emergencies; the arrest bit may be made up | Real lesson: police come to school for every call | It proves prank calls are funny if you hang up | It is gossip, so prank calls do no harm
test: my cousin reckons u can't call 911 if ur phone is locked or has no credit | Wrong; emergency calls are free and most phones can call the number even when locked | He is right, you need credit for 911 | He is right, you must unlock it first | He is right, unless you are an adult
test: Maddie called 000 when her nan fainted and people are saying she overreacted, should i say that too | No, calling for help when someone faints is the right thing to do | Yes, because nans faint often | Yes, so she does not call again | Yes, but only in the group chat
exam: Which of the following constitutes the most important information to be conveyed to an emergency call taker? | The location at which the caller is situated | The caller's age and favourite subject | The colour of the caller's clothing | The name of the caller's school teacher
exam: It being given that an emergency number has been dialled in error, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To remain on the line and state that the call was a mistake | To hang up immediately without speaking | To switch off the phone entirely | To call again repeatedly to cancel it
exam: Identify the emergency number applicable in New Zealand. | One hundred and eleven (111) | Nine hundred and eleven (911) | Nine hundred and ninety-nine (999) | Triple zero (000)
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance where the caller does not know the address. | To describe nearby landmarks such as shops, signs or parks | To end the call and search for the address | To guess any address in the town | To decline to give any location whatsoever
